  <branding>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="light">#deddda</color>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="dark">#3d3846</color>
  </branding>
  <content_rating type="oars-1.1"/>
  <releases>
    <release version="0.2.2" date="2026-11-27">
      <description>
        <p>New temperature charts. Baseboard still only reads: it does not change any setting.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The five-minute charts use one scale of 0 to 100 °C on every card, with degree labels and the limit of each part where it is known, so the same temperature stands at the same height</li>
          <li>A clearer line and fill, with the range of the last five minutes beside the temperature</li>
          <li>In a larger window the charts grow with it, within limits, and stay wider than tall</li>
          <li>On AMD processors the processor's chart no longer has gaps for the time the window was hidden</li>
          <li>The range of fan speeds on the Fans tile stays on one line</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Fan control comes in a later version.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.2.1" date="2026-11-16">
      <description>
        <p>Small improvements after 0.2.0. Baseboard still only reads: it does not change any setting.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>In a large window, the five-minute charts on the Overview and Fans pages grow to fill the free space, up to a square, instead of leaving the bottom of the window empty; windows of 1200 × 800 and smaller look as before</li>
          <li>Diagnostics is now a single list, like in GNOME Settings</li>
          <li>Icons for disks, wired and Wi-Fi network cards on the System page</li>
          <li>The status icon is now centred in its row</li>
          <li>Screen readers now read the value and subtitle of each row and announce each new warning once</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Fan control comes in a later version.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.2.0" date="2026-11-14">
      <description>
        <p>A new look and a Fans page. Baseboard still only reads: it does not change any setting.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A new Overview: the processor, graphics card and board with their temperature over the last five minutes, and the fans, memory and disks at a glance</li>
          <li>A Fans page with the speed and duty cycle of every fan and, where the kernel driver reports them, the curves the board runs the fans by</li>
          <li>A new System page, short like About in GNOME Settings, with the details one click away</li>
          <li>Warnings when the processor gets very hot, when the CPU fan stops and when a voltage is outside its tolerance</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Fan control comes in a later version.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.1.0" date="2026-11-11">
      <description>
        <p>The first release. Baseboard reads the sensors of a motherboard and shows information about the system; it does not control anything yet.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Temperatures, fan speeds and voltages; on a board with a profile, named after its fan headers and voltage rails</li>
          <li>Voltages rated against their tolerance, with the lowest and highest value since the app started</li>
          <li>A System page with the board, BIOS, processor, memory, graphics card, disks and the fan driver, which can be copied as text for bug reports</li>
          <li>English and Polish</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Fan control, onboard RGB lighting and power profiles are not there yet.</p>
      </description>
    </release>
  </releases>
</component>
